When I asked this question I was given to understand that all the Alpha
workstations currently sold can run VMS, WNT, or Unix. It's just a
question of having the right OS licenses & system disks. The 3000 series
is not currently sold btw; it cannot do this. Also I believe Multia is not
classed as a workstation. That leaves a fair number of others however,
most of which are faster than the old Jensen.

I cannot say much about pricing or bundling of licenses, but you can
certainly buy dual or even tri-OS machines. 

As for getting them to read one another's disks, you may be better off having
a tape drive to swap things. Intraco Systems has a VMS filestructure reading
system for DEC Unix, and PCDISK (part of Pathworks) reads and writes msdos
floppies (and maybe other msdos type filesystems), and similar packages
exist for unix, but for the moment it's tough to get general disk
access across OSs on one machine. Techniques like using NFS or DFS work
only where multiple CPUs are available each running different OSs. This
may be a consideration, depending on what you're trying to do.

(There is a utility to read VMS filesystems on recent sig tapes too; it
can be used, free, and should be buildable on NT as it was on DEC Unix.)



