In article <4gtkp0$7lu@overload.lbl.gov>, 
tonyk@sseos.lbl.gov (Tony Konashenok) writes...

>Does anyone have a configuration table for LN03S? Got one for free,
>and it is supposed to work, but someone must have played with the
>little switches in the back, so it seems to require more modem control
>signals than I can provide...

    Here's what it says in the LNO3 Programmer Reference Manual (hope it
    applies to the LN03S) ...

    Looking at the back of the printer, there are two switch packs with six
    switches in each.  The "up" position is "on" or "1".

                   SP1                        SP2
        +---+---+---+---+---+---+  +---+---+---+---+---+---+
        | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 |  | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 |
        +---+---+---+---+---+---+  +---+---+---+---+---+---+
          |  \_________/  |   |      |  \_____/  |   |   |
          |       |       |   |      |     |     |   |   +- restraint
          |       |       |   |      |     |     |   |      polarity
          |       |       |   |      |     |     |   +- restraint protocol
          |       |       |   |      |     |     +- autowrap enable
          |       |       |   |      |     +- device ID select
          |       |       |   |      +- parity set
          |       |       |   +- parity enable
          |       |       +- 7/8 data bits format
          |       +- BAUD rate select
          +- interface type

    SP1<1> interface type, 1=parallel, 0=serial
    SP1<4:2> BAUD rate, 000=1200
                        001=2400
                        010=3600
                        011=4800
                        100=7200
                        101=9600
                        110=19200
                        111=19200
    SP1<5> 7/8 bits data format, 1=7 bits, 0=8 bits
    SP1<6> parity enable, 1=enabled, 0=disabled

    SP2<1> parity set, 1=space (odd) parity, 0=mark (even) parity
    SP2<3:2> device ID, 00=LN03
                        01=LA100
                        10=LQP02
                        11=LN03
    SP2<4> autowrap enable, 1=wrap, 0=no wrap
    SP2<5> restraint protocol, 1=restraint, 0=XON/XOFF
    SP2<6> restraint polarity, 1=inverted, 0=normal


    The document says that if you place the printer off-line and press the
    test switch, the current state of the configuration switches is
    printed.  Also important to remember is that the switches are only read
    at power-on, so you need to cycle power whenever you change the
    switches.

    Please excuse any typos in the above,

    -- Kenny

