Subject: Re: Can users mount/umount CDs in DEC Unix 3.2c?
Date: 1996/02/27
Message-ID: <RJM.96Feb27112758@swift.eng.ox.ac.uk>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 141496444
distribution: world
references: <LOU.96Feb26123507@sun1.giac.montana.edu>
organization: Oxford University Engineering Department
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

In some article lou@sun1.giac.montana.edu (lou) writes:

   Is there a way that a user (not root) can mount and 
   unmount a CD?  The reason we wanted CD's is to enable
   our users to access large geographic data sets which
   come in CD form.

Maybe possible. Depends on DEC UNIX (I'm a Linux man myself, but I'm
here since I have a PDP and a microVAX to feed and water at home too
;)
   Right now, my reading of mount(1) is that 

    "To use the mount and unmount commands, you must be root."

Possibly (usually) true - unless you have a nice version of mount.

   I have tried:

	   [1] as root, chown the mount command to the user.

	   result: user cannot mount a cd.

That will _never_ work. Unless the binary is setUID then its ownership
will change nothing - other than who can execute it according to the
permissions.

	   [2] run root-setuid script to do [1].

	   result: user cannot mount a cd.

Generally, for security reasons, setUID scripts do not set the UID ;)
You could use a little C program to do it though. Just use the exec()
system call to call mount with the appropriate switches. Make the C
program setUID root and you should be OK. If you're not too careful
with your code, this could be a security hole. SetUID scripts are
considered just too dangerous.

	   [3] chmod the cd device to 666.

	   result: user cannot mount a cd.

mount needs to be able to write to /etc/mtab and also notify the
kernel, so having permissions on the device is not enough.

	   [4] as root, chown the cd device to the user.

	   result: user cannot mount a cd.

See my comments for [3]

   The last (untried) bletchery I will try, is to run a 
   cron job as root (say, every 10 min) that will check
   for a 'mount-request' file in the /tmp directory, and, 
   if it's there, perform a mount of the cd ... and likewise
   for an 'unmount-request'... This is awkward, at best.

nasty! Should work though ...

I recommend you carefully peruse the mount(1) man page. Linux allows
an oprion (user) in /etc/fstab to allow user mounting of devices,
precisely for this purpose. Failing that the little C program approach
may be the best bet: remember to make it suid root. (chown root.root
foo; chmod u+s foo)

Cheers,
Bob.

