>Kevin Weller wrote:
>> 
>> Despite my embarrassment, I must say that the good folks at DEC sure
>> buried the "atom" toolkit deep.  It's man pages weren't included in
>> the standard places, so I had absolutely no idea it existed.  Only
>> after someone with Digital tech support mentioned it, along with
>> several nice people via email, did I actually know of it...but not
>> even the DEC tech could tell me where the documentation was or how to
>> use it!  I wound up searching the entire "dxbook" CD-ROM for
>> references to the word "atom", finally turning up some documentation
>> on where the stuff is and how to use it.  But it was too late, since I

The Atom toolkit shipped as an Advanced Developer's Kit on Digital Unix
3.0 and later systems.  It will be supplied as a fully supported product
on Digital Unix 4.0.

Because Atom was not a fully supported product on earlier Digital Unix
systems, it was supplied as an optional subset on the base operating 
system CD.  The subset name is ATMBASE3??, where ?? depends on the
operating system release.  The subset contains reference pages and
several manuals as postscript files.

The ADK contains a prototype version of the Atom software and
documentation.  Because it wasn't fully supported, documentation on
the kit's availability was limited to the release notes that
shipped with the system.

In Digital Unix 4.0, to be released in the near future, the reference
pages for Atom are included with other base system documentation.
Also, the Programmer's Guide has been updated to contain new chapters
on building Atom tools and the Atom tools that are packaged with the
OSFSDE (Development Environment) subset.

I realize this information is too late to help with the C++ problem,
but hopefully it will be of use to you in the future.




