>> nearly) reach the needed speed.

John> We are interested in the answer to this particular question
John> (3000/600 vs.  2100 5/250) as well, but with the following
John> particulars:

John> We use our 3000/600 as a timesharing system (often with as many
John> as a 100 simultaneous users), who mostly do things like pine,
John> telnet, and lynx.  The unit is also the main campus Web server,
John> though I don't think that yet cuts into its performance
John> significantly.  It is also, however, the campus NNTP News
John> server, and that probably does eat up some power.

John> Anyway, we need to expand our operation to provide more
John> timesharing resources, and are trying to decide between a
John> 3000/600 and an Alpha 2100 such as the one mentioned above.  The
John> problem we are having in making the decision is that while many
John> specs exist for the raw CPU power of the respective machines,
John> very little is available on the I/O bandwidth.  This would have
John> to do with things like the speed of the "backplane", and there
John> also the question of CPU cache size and TB (translation buffer)
John> size.

Well, the 2100 has a backplane that does around 664Mb/sec, has three
PCI slots at 132Mb/s and 8 EISA slots at 32Mb/s.  The 2100A is the
same, except the numbers of slots PCI vs. EISA is reversed.  Much
nicer numbers.

What I would do is go around looking for a used 2100 4/200 and then
buy a few extra CPU boards and a bunch of memory.  Viola, a reasonably
cheap and powerful SMP system with room for expansion.  

John> A notable data point is that we have both a 3000/600 and a
John> 3000/300LX (which I am told has VAXstation 4000 VLC class I/O
John> technology).  The specs suggest relative chip speeeds of 125 for
John> the 300LX and 175 for the 600.  But with equivalent memory, the
John> 600 is MUCH MUCH faster when a lot of users are on, and I'm sure
John> this is due to a faster I/O bus, as well as 2MB instead of 0.5MB
John> CPU cache, and a larger TB.

John> So what I want to know is, how can I expect the 2100 to perform
John> under multi-user load?  What does it have for CPU cache figures,
John> IO bandwidth capacity, and TB sizes?

The 2100 will perform better than the 3000/600 with an equivalent
number of users and RAM installed in the system.  This assumes the
5/250, but even the 4/200 should be a little bit faster than the
3000/600.

You can also look at the Systems and Options Catalog on the Web for
more details.  http://www.digital.com/info/SOC/

