> In article <1996Feb21.141303.91@wvml>,
>         psmode@wvml.jeslacs.bc.ca (Peter A. Smode) writes:
> > I have a RRD42 attached to my VAXstation 4000 running VMS v5.5-2. I also have a
> > shiny new AlphaStation running Digital Unix. Along with all this, I have the
> > Online Doc set for Unix. On the VMS system I have UCX. I have no InfoServer.
> > 
> > Since I don't currently have a CD on the AlphaStation, is there any way I can
> > mount the Unix Online Doc CD on the VAXstation and read the docs with the VMS
> > bookreader? Can I serve the CD from the VAX to the Unix system?
> > 

> How about using NFS? I understand that the current version of UCX has an NFS
> server.

The problem with this is that NFS is a file-level serving protocol.  That is,
it only knows how to serve volumes that contain file systems that the
underlying OS understands.  Thus, since I believe the OLD Doc CDs for
DEC UNIX are in UFS format, there is no way for VMS to mount them, and hence
no way for the NFS server to serve them.  The reverse is true for Digital
UNIX and Files-11.

What I want to know is why aren't BOTH of these CD sets now written in ISO
format that CAN be read by both Operating Systems?

- John W. Manly  <JWMANLY@AMHERST.EDU>




