>we are using a DEC PDP 11/34 computer to write processed data from a CT 
>scanner onto 9 track tape unit. We are interested on writing the data onto
>4 mm. tape unit. I will appreciate any suggestion.
>
>some one has suggested me the following solution:
><a> 4 mm. tape unit EXB-8200 external tape drive(2048 mb)
><b>CDU-720/T Unibus SCSI tape controller(Probably needs some kind of MT
>type driver)

The above combination would be an excellent choice (though I didn't
know that EXB-8200's were still being sold as new), if it wasn't
for what you say below:

>*****this DEC PDP 11/34 computer runs RSX-11m version 3 software.

RSX-11M v3.0-3.2 do not include support for MU: (TMSCP) type devices,
which is the sort of tape device a CDU-720 is.  The first support for 
MU: type devices appeared in RSX-11M v4.0 or v4.1, if I remember
correctly.

Now there are two things that you could do:

1.  Upgrade to a recent version of RSX-11M or 11M+ which will
support the CMD SCSI controller.  Depending on how tied you
are to specific device drivers and applications, this may
or may not be a possibility.

2.  Find a controller that will let you hook a Exabyte up in
MS: or MT: emulation.  I do know that there are Exabytes with
formatted Pertec interfaces, probably being done through
a Pertec -> SCSI converter.  And a MS: (TS11) emulating Pertec
formatted controller for a 11/34 is easy to find.  You may
still have to do a SYSGEN from your RSX-11M distribution kit
to get support for the MS: driver.

Tim.



