From: shoppa@altair.krl.caltech.edu (Tim Shoppa)
Subject: Re: What do I replace RD54 with?
Date: 1996/02/21
Message-ID: <4ggb7o$jun@gap.cco.caltech.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 140482740
references: <4gfk1f$j8c@hobbes.crc.com>
organization: Kellogg Radiation Lab, Caltech
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

In article <4gfk1f$j8c@hobbes.crc.com>, Kevin P. Inscoe <kpi@crc.com> wrote:
>Hello VAX managers,
>
>I have been out of the VAX world for almost 4 years now and my experience was 
>with large systems (11/750+) back then. So bear with me if the question I ask
>seems dumb.
>
>I have one lone VAX that I manage (actually I don't touch it but maybe twice a 
>year) and it is a MVII (model 630QB-A2, circa. 1987) and it is the office kind 
>on rollers (not the pedestal kind). It looks sorta like a large 11/725. Anyway
>it has one lone RD54 as the boot and user disk. This disk only has 311200 
>blocks total and with VMS it is getting quite crowded. I have tailored off
>everything I can.
>
>So anyway I need to add an additional disk because I need ~100K blocks 
>additional space.
>
>They don't want to spend more then about ~$500 for this because the VAX has 
>only one user and eventually he will migrate to a PC. So what do you kind folks 
>recommend? Can I add another RD54 to the controller

Yes, this is certainly an option.  Formatted RD54's are available
for $300-$500.  In particular, you can be then from DEC's Refurbished
Equipment Group for $300 (DEC Part no. B2-RD54A-BA).

The RD54 is a Maxtor 2190 which has been formatted for use on your
controller.  You may be able to find 2190's for substantially less than
$300, but you'd have to format them on the VAX before use.  And you
almost certainly don't have the Microvax Field Service tape with
the formatter.

> or should I replace the 
>RD54 with another compatable larger disk (and if so what?).

As far as I know, the RD54/Maxtor 2190 is as large as MFM drives got.

> I took off the 
>cover and the RD54 is connected to module M9058. 

The M9058 is just a distribution board.

>
>What kind of controller is this? 

The M9058 is connected to the actual controller, a RQDX3 (M7555).

>There is space above for annother RD54 
>connection but do I need to jumper anything or make any changes? Will the 
>controller "auto detect" the new drive?

Yes, the controller will auto-detect a formatted RD54 connected to
it.  You will have to still build a VMS directory structure on the
new disk with VMS's INIT command.

>If I replace this drive will it be compatible with the controller?

Yes, if it has been properly formatted.  But you won't be able to
replace it with a larger drive, as there are no larger drives available.

If you wanted a larger drive, you might think about getting a Q-bus
SCSI controller and a SCSI disk.  This combo might run you anywhere
between $500 and $2000, depending on whether you get an older SCSI
controller or a brand new one.  Older SCSI controllers may not
be compatible with VMS.

For more details about RQDX3's and RD54's, be sure to check out the file
THIRD-PARTY-DISKS.TXT, available by anonymous FTP from the
top-level anonymous directory on ftp.spc.edu.

Tim. (shoppa@altair.krl.caltech.edu)



