Kevin Weller wrote:
> 
> Despite my embarrassment, I must say that the good folks at DEC sure
> buried the "atom" toolkit deep.  It's man pages weren't included in
> the standard places, so I had absolutely no idea it existed.  Only
> after someone with Digital tech support mentioned it, along with
> several nice people via email, did I actually know of it...but not
> even the DEC tech could tell me where the documentation was or how to
> use it!  I wound up searching the entire "dxbook" CD-ROM for
> references to the word "atom", finally turning up some documentation
> on where the stuff is and how to use it.  But it was too late, since I

If you go into /usr/.smdb. and grep through the *.inv files for
things like *.ps and *.txt you'll find where the release
notes and such are buried along with what subsets they're in. The
*.inv files are the "inventory" files for the currently loaded and
"not loaded but looked at" subsets. I look in there pretty often to 
a) find out where libraries are loaded from so I can get the right
   subset installed.
b) reload a library from the CD-ROM if it gets corrupted for any reason
c) snoop around looking for release notes so I don't waste my time 
   trying to fix documented bugs.
d) find out where all the related files for a tool are loaded.

setld will sometimes tell you where the release notes are when the 
subset is loaded, but many times a sysadmin is the one who loads the
subset and they probably can't tell everyone where the release notes
are.
