Yves Choquette <choquett> wrote:
>A year ago, we bought a DEC 3000 Model 600S AXP Workstation.  Actualy, we are
>looking for a server who is at least twice faster.  A look at the specs tells
>me that an AlphaServer 2100 5/250 would provide us a lot more than twice the
>speed needed.  However, a sale representative told me that i would barely (but
>nearly) reach the needed speed.

We are interested in the answer to this particular question (3000/600 vs.
2100 5/250) as well, but with the following particulars:

We use our 3000/600 as a timesharing system (often with as many as a 100
simultaneous users), who mostly do things like pine, telnet, and lynx.  The
unit is also the main campus Web server, though I don't think that yet cuts
into its performance significantly.  It is also, however, the campus NNTP
News server, and that probably does eat up some power.

Anyway, we need to expand our operation to provide more timesharing
resources, and are trying to decide between a 3000/600 and an Alpha 2100
such as the one mentioned above.  The problem we are having in making
the decision is that while many specs exist for the raw CPU power of the
respective machines, very little is available on the I/O bandwidth.  This
would have to do with things like the speed of the "backplane", and there
also the question of CPU cache size and TB (translation buffer) size.

A notable data point is that we have both a 3000/600 and a 3000/300LX
(which I am told has VAXstation 4000 VLC class I/O technology).  The 
specs suggest relative chip speeeds of 125 for the 300LX and 175 for
the 600.  But with equivalent memory, the 600 is MUCH MUCH faster when a
lot of users are on, and I'm sure this is due to a faster I/O bus, as
well as 2MB instead of 0.5MB CPU cache, and a larger TB.

So what I want to know is, how can I expect the 2100 to perform under
multi-user load?  What does it have for CPU cache figures, IO bandwidth
capacity, and TB sizes?

Thanks for any help, and if I still haven't provided enough info about the
kinds of work the system needs to do, just let me know.

- John W. Manly  <JWMANLY@AMHERST.EDU>




