Greetings:

  I've been trying to resolve this problem since November of last year
without any luck, maybe someone can add something...


The System:

  A VAX 4000-100A system with 32 meg of memory and the Q-Bus expansion option.  
For those not familiar with it, that's a 4000-100A chassis (CPU & memory) 
connected to a mVAX II BA-123 chassis via 2 cables and adapter cards which 
replace the mVAX II CPU and memory.  Everything's been working well for the 
last 2 years.


Software:

Standard from the box;  OpenVMS 5.5-2, DECNet, LAT, Fortran, C, DBase IV
3rd party network;      CMU TCP/IP 6.6-5A, MX 4.1, NewsReader 4.8, 
                        Lynx 2-3-3 beta, LIBCMU (?), NETLIB 1.6(?)
installed but disabled; IUPOP3, OSU Web Server.

  Last software installed was OSU Web Server (July) however the VAX was 
manually re-booted several times between July and November without any 
problems.  All other 3rd party network software has been on and running for 
the last 12 to 24 months without problems.  With the exception of Fortran,
C and DBase updates, the basic VMS software hasn't been touched since 
before the 4000 upgrade.


Problem:

  Since the middle of November, the VAX has been bugchecking (crashing).  The 
longest it's been up has been a month, the shortest, 20 minutes.  Usually 2 
days to a week.  Load doesn't appear to play a part.  It's crashed with and 
without users connected, the system in use or sitting idle.  Oddly, it seems 
to only crash between 6am and 6pm Monday thru Friday.


Investigations:

  While not an expert in the use of SDA, all the crashes appear nearly 
identical to me, and the system appears to be crashing while executing code 
in the VMS network driver (EZDRIVER).  If I understand the SDA output correctly,
it looks like the driver is attempting to access a null page in memory, which 
by design, causes the system to bugcheck.  The data is very similar to one of 
the examples in the VMS 5.5 Grey-Wall SDA manual.  However, I had expected to 
see the Signal Array Program Counter pointing to a non-VMS piece of code.  The
only thing I can guess might be happening is either the driver is receiving 
bad data from some other piece of software, or something unexpected is coming
in from the network and the driver is unable to process it?

  SDA excerpts are included at the end of this posting.


Actions Taken:

  My initial assumption was that I had a non-VMS software, or hardware problem, 
and I attempted to isolate it through the following actions:

  1)  Shutdown Web Server and IUPOP3 software.  No change.

  2)  Switched ethernet connection from Q-Bus DELQA card to internal 4000 
      ethernet adapter.  No change except crash now occured in EZDRIVER
      instead of XQDRIVER. (Only have an End-Node license)

  3)  Swapped external ethernet cable and tranceiver with one from a different
      machine.  No change.

  4)  Added additional 16 meg of memory (now at 32 meg).  No change.

  5)  Replaced lower 16 meg of memory.  No change.

  6)  Verified enough space on system disk (~500 meg) for file growth.

  7)  Found MOM.LOG was at version 32767.  Purged and renamed back to 1.
      No change.

  8)  No errors reported by firmware diagnostics when starting the system.

  I'm at a loss now as to what to try next.  As we haven't felt the need to 
carry hardware maintenance on this machine (we've never had an unexplainable 
VAX hardware/software failure), this isn't an option at the moment.  In any
case, it's so intermittent, an on-site service call probably wouldn't help.

  I came across a patch the other day in the Digital service patch area,
VAXPHV10_U2055, which seems to address the symptoms (VMS 5.5-2, ethernet
driver problems which could cause a system crash).  However I'm reluctant
to install it without having some idea of what's wrong and/or assurance 
that it would correct the problem.

  Any thoughts or suggestions greatly appreciated!  

