$ cre/dir dkb200:[rsx_root]
$ assign/trans=conc dkb200:[rsx_root.] $$1:

RSX device names are two ASCII characters and a binary number expressed
in octal.  Some VMS device names have VAX-11 RSX equivalents, which I won't
get into here.  VAX-11 RSX supports VMS logical names of two dollars and 
a number pointing to any device or disk directory tree.  In the above 
example, VAX-11 RSX thinks $$0: is a tape drive, and thinks the directory
tree $$1: points to is a disk.

VAX-11 RSX also supplies an extended interface for programs that link with
an extended FCS or RMS, but it will probably be easier to run the old .TSK
file with $$ names than to rebuild the program on VMS.

Try DSCCPY with $$1 pointing to a directory tree on an ODS-2 (ordinary VMS)
disk.  Most .TSK files will work.  If not, you will need an ODS-1 volume. 
If you have a spare disk, you can $ INIT DKB200: /STRUC=1 on a VAX (not 
supported on Alpha VMS) and use it.  If you don't have a spare disk, look
for Glenn Everhart's VMS virtual disk drivers on almost any recent VMS
SIGtape and create a virtual disk in a container file.

If you get stuck, feel free to call me.

Good luck,
Tony Scandora, Argonne National Lab, 708-252-7541
scandora@cmt.anl.gov

P.S.  Thanks, Tim, for pointing to Sunsite as an archive of old RSX SIGtapes.
I never would have guessed to look there!



