jwatkins <jwatkins@intellinet.com> wrote:
>I am writing a communications program for DEC/UNIX running on an 
>DEC/ALPHA something or other.  The problem I am having is that I 
>cannot get the modem to sync up at 2400,e,7,1 to talk to a 
>mainframe (they wont change their setting for me).  The modem is 
>hooked up to /dev/tty01, which when you do a man on scc(7) you 
>get this:
>	  
>	  In alternate console configurations (that is, no 
>graphics head), communica-
>	  tions port 2 (/dev/tty01) becomes the system console. 
>The serial system
>	  console port always operates at 9600 BPS, 8-bits, no 
>parity.  No modem con-
>	  trol is supported.   
>
>Since we have no graphics display hooked up, does that mean that 
>I must get the modem moved?  

Instead of trying to change the port characteristics, you might let the 
modem do speed shifting.  Assuming that you are using an AT type modem, 
try either ATF5D or ATN0S37=6D for your dial command.  Assuming that 
your modem supports either of these commands, it forces the connection 
between modems to occur at 2400 baud.

You may need flow control between the Alpha and the modem if you are 
sending data to the modem at a higher rate.

