neill@macro.demon.co.uk (Neill Clift) wrote:
>Well I have two questions. One about the uVAX 2000 and the other about the 3100
>M38. First the 2000:

[snip]

I don't know the full answer to this one, but I do know that the 
difference between a MV2000 and a VS2000 is (surprise!) one jumper on 
the motherboard.  It sounds like you found this jumper.

The Ethernet interface consists of some logic on the motherboard and a 
separate daughterboard that is connected by ribbon cables.  I had a 
VS2000 that wasn't showing the Ethernet as present.  It turned out that 
one of the ribbon cables was in backwards.  Turning it around fixed it.


>Now on to the 3100 M38. This recent aquisition to Neill's VAX museum I though
>was really begging for a little floppy disk so I ordered one (RX23) from a
>broker. I told this guy I wanted it for the M38 and I would need the bracket
>to fit it onto the metal plate and the plastic face plate for the machine with
>the right sized hole. the drive arrived but the plate that came with it just
>doesn't fit and the RX23 doesn't have a proper scsi connecter on the back (Its
>got a number of RX23-A on it). Looking at the bookreader doco for the machine
>it seems like this drive should sit on another metal plate that contains a scsi
>connecter and a power connecter that I assume should fit into the drive itself
>when its attached. Anyone know exactly how this is set up so I can get this guy
>to send me the right thing?

There are two versions of the RX23. One has a SCSI interface, the other 
has a floppy interface.  It sounds like you got the later.  That drive 
requires the SCSI/ST506 controller board.

As far as I know, all M38s shipped with the SCSI/SCSI controller board 
and used the SCSI variant of the RX23.

Also, the original (SCSI/ST506) RX23 didn't have a face plate on the 
drive.  The bezel on the VS3100 had just a slot for inserting the 
floppy and a hole for the eject button.

The SCSI RX23 has a faceplate on the drive and the bezel has a 
rectangular cutout which exposes the drive.

Hope this helps!

