Yes, I use an AlphaStation 400 4/233 which was ordered with VMS and I later ordered
and installed NT.  There were some difficulties with firmware for the SRM (VMS/unix) vs.
ARC (NT) consoles when installing NT 3.5 and upgrading to 3.51.  I found that the
firmware updates available at Micro$oft were current for ARC, but not always current
for SRM; as a result, I had to either run only one of the O/S, or use my firmware BACKUP
to step back to the previous state.  I found that the VMS distribution kit (CDs) had
firmware updates that allowed both (new) versions to run (had a similar problem when 
upgrading OpenVMS 6.-1h2 => 6.2).  The firmware I'm currently using (NTv3.51sp2 and
OpenVMSv6.2) is Firmware Update v3.2 from the May'95 VMS CD kit.  My last FW update
was in May'95, so I don't know which are the best sources for NT 4 (which I will get).

The two-O/S setup works well.  It takes less than 3 minutes to go from initiating shutdown 
on one O/S to the login dialog box on the other.  The only problem (i.e. pain) is you have to 
redefine the time/date every time (NT = dd-mm-yy and VMS = dd-mmm-yyyy).  This seems 
stupid, especially when going from VMS to NT: the console screen where you have to type 
in the NT time/date format has the current NT time/date being displayed in the upper 
left corner.  It shouldn't take a lot of coding to make the format shift automatic.

