Bus performace of DSSI and CI is about the same, and DSSI is a lot cheaper
because you don't need star couplers, HSCs or equivalents to HSCs.

However, DSSI disk is basically obsolescent, so you really should stick with
Digital or 3rd party DSSI-SCSI converters for your storage.

Other writers have suggested FDDI.  This has a signficant drawback over DSSI in
that it uses a lot more CPU time for the same workload.  DSSI is functionally
very close to CI (send messages, send blocks of data without cpu intervention,
interrupt the other cpu, etc) while most of this functionality in FDDI and
Ethernet has to be emulated in software.  the biggest emulation workload is in
copying blocks of data through network buffers, while DSSI and CI go direct.

The main drawback to DSSI is that the limit is 3 nodes, and only then provided
the middle node has the correct pair of DSSI connectors.  (OK, 3 nodes on the
same DSSI bus...you can have dozens if you go CI, Ethernet, or FDDI).

Sorry, no horror stories...I've successfully avoided troubles by doing the
right things!!!! However, I can tell you about some other guys...


Good luck

John




