In article <4g5p6m$jnp$1@mhade.production.compuserve.com>, Brian Roth <73541.3045@CompuServe.COM> writes:
>    Does anyone know whether a TU-80 controller will operate a 
> pertec interface tape drive such as the Cipher F-880. The 
> interface "looks" pertec, but I wanted some feedback before I went 
> and toasted a controller board.

  The answer is "probably". Neither the TU80 controller (M7454) nor the F880
are quite standard formatted Pertec.

  The TU80 controller only supports a single drive and has some other firm-
ware oddities. It's the same hardware as a Dilog DU13x, so if you put Dilog
ROMs in it you get a vanilla controller.

  The F880 has a few oddities of its own. First, it has to be told by the
controller what speed (IPS) to operate at. Next, most units won't report an
ID burst at BOT on 3200BPI tapes (if your unit does 3200 and you want to use
it). There's logic on the DQ132 (and thus, presumably on the TU80 control-
ler) that gets upset if it doesn't see the PE Burst signal. There's a hack
floating around for the DQ132 to simulate a PE Burst whenever BOT is cros-
sed. Since the F880 doesn't do 800BPI, this is harmless.

  In summary, you won't blow anything up, but you may have other problems.

