Message-ID: <4g6c5f$rfr@pt9201.ped.pto.ford.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 139887858
references: <4g0th6$hff@news.isl.net>
organization: Ford Motor Co., Powertrain Electronics
reply-to: fuller@pt9113.ped.pto.ford.com
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

In article <4g0th6$hff@news.isl.net>, dreif@isl.net writes:
>VAX Experts,
>
>Is there a known problem with attaching large capacity SCSI drives to the
>internal SCSI bus of a VAX 4000 Model 60?
>
>However, on the VAX 4000 Model 60, he gets a "Fatal Drive Error" when he
>first tries to access it.  

This is most commonly due to the drive has the ARWE and/or ARRE bits set in the
error control page.  These bits determine the drive's response to a hard write
or read error.  If the bit is on, then a hard error will result in the drive
revector the bad block to a spare block.  VMS doesn't want the drive to do
this; it wants to do the revectoring on its own.

However, looking at the error log will indicate exactly what VMS is complaining
about.

>If it makes any difference, the customer is running VMS 5.5.2.  Though my
>understanding is that the errors he gets happen way before VMS is loaded
>from his boot hard drive.

Well, VMS must have booted for it to tell you of the "Fatal drive error".

It's my understanding that VMS V6.2 relaxes this restriction, and will allow
you to use a drive with the ARWE/ARRE bits on.

