>    running  RSX-11M.   (That "DSC" could be "DCS", since he  remembered
>    words like "disk compress and  save",  but  "DSC"  is  what's  hand-
>    written  on  the  tape  label.)

It is "DSC" - "Disk save and compress".

>  This  looks  to  be a 9-track tape,
>    probably written at 800 bpi.  I  have  not yet attempted to mount it
>    under VMS.
>
>        Question: does anyone have a  program  that runs on VMS that can
>    read  a DCS tape?

While there is a DECUS VMS program that can read RSX-11M BRU tapes,
I am not aware of a VMS program that will read DSC tapes.

>  We no longer have access to an RSX-11M (or  other
>    PDP-11) system.  If not, and if I were to install  the  RSX  layered
>    product  on  VMS,  would  that  allow  me  to read the tape?  (No, I
>    haven't talked to DEC yet, nor tried  to  see  if I can even get the
>    RSX compatibility product any more...)

Yes, the RSX compatibility product will read and write DSC tapes.
Rather than try to buy a modern version, you might want to find
a system running v3.x or v4.x VMS which will almost certainly
have the RSX compatibility software on it already.  (Note:
it must be v4.x VMS, not Micro-VMS.)  Alternatively,
you can find a RSX-11M system with a 800 bpi drive on it, and
Kermit the results out (or physically move a disk between
the RSX system and a VMS system, as RSX disks that are smaller
than 256 Megabytes can be mounted on VMS.)

Tim. (shoppa@altair.krl.caltech.edu)



