In article <4ft87n$7ev$1@mhadf.production.compuserve.com>, Carla Reisch
<74472.3210@CompuServe.COM> wrote:
>I am posting this message on my brother's behalf.  He would like to 
>transfer some text files from his IBM PC (486) to a laser machine 
>which uses a DEC PDP 11/23+ computer.  The DEC only has an 8" floppy
>and a hard drive.  He could also communicate through the RS232 
>serial port.  Has anyone written some transfer software so that data
>could be transferred from the PC to the DEC via RS232?  (Preferably 
>written in PASCAL since that's what he uses on the laser machine.)  
>You can e-mail my brother, Robert, with suggestions at 
>rjreisch@ccgate.hac.com or simply reply to this post and I will 
>forward it to him.

Run Kermit, Windows Terminal, or any other half-decent terminal emulator
on the PC, and connect it in place of the 11/23 CONSOLE.  You can then
I believe do .COPY TT0: filnam.ext as a DEC command (I assume it runs
similarly to DEC RT-11), and then cause the emulator to send a PC file,
terminating from keyboard with whatever terminates TT0: input.  I have
assuredly done the reverse.  There will be no comms checking, but it
should be reliable and you can always do it thrice and compare.

You can probably also connect the PC to any other serial port that the
11/23 has an inwards device driver for.
-- 
