This is kind of a repost of a problem I posted about several weeks
ago, involving a Sony SDT-5000 DAT drive, which I thought had been
resolved but actually wasn't... and now doesn't appear to be specific
to a particular brand of drive either.

The problem was originally that I couldn't get Digital Unix 3.2 to
recognize the drive on an AXP 3000/400. After more than one reboot,
the OS started automatically creating the right special files for the
drive at startup, except that mt status would not show that a tape was
online until I explicitly gave an "mt load" command from inside
scu(8). With that drive, I only needed to do this once --- the next
tape would automatically come online when inserted, and the next, at
least until the next reboot.

Well, that drive ate a cleaning tape so we sent it back. Now we have a
DEC TLZ04 as a loaner --- and guess what, it behaves the same way!
Even worse actually, because I have to become root and load *every*
tape by hand from scu. To put it another way, anyone can do an mt
unload on a tape I've brought online, but I still have to go through
scu all over again the next time someone wants to load a tape.

I think I've checked the obvious stuff (e.g. the special files are
writable by anyone, not just root; there's also an entry for TLZ04 in
/usr/sys/data/cam_data.c). I've talked to a sysadm at a collaborating
department who has much more experience than I with OSF/1, and he's
stumped too.

Any suggestions, advice, etc. would be much appreciated...

