> Our project is currently planning on porting FORTRAN applications from old
> VAX 11/750 to DEC 3000-900s and 700s.  These machines contain Turbo
> Channels and we have a requirement to control devices on a VME bus. 
> 
> We have located a company, BIT3, to provide a Turbo Channel to VME bus
> adaptor but their driver is no longer supported and will not run on OpenVMS
> 7.0 (they stopped supporting it at version 6.1).  BIT3 claims that the
> Turbo Channel is dead?  What's the scoop?

Scoop 1: You need to push RETURN occasionally so you don't wind up with
paragraphs on a single line.

Scoop 2: The last order date for Alphas having TURBOchannel has been
extended to the end of March. Last ship date is projected for June. 
Although DEC would clearely prefer you buy PCI machines, this is (IIRC) the 
second time the last order date has been extended. I guess there's still 
demand for them.

Scoop 3: TURBOchannel remains the only way to expand the VAXstations, if 
you can get your hands on a TURBOchannel adapter. DEC's working on the
problem.

Scoop 4: TURBOchannel is much cooler than PCI, IMHO. A minimal TURBOchannel 
interface requires a PAL and a rank of registered transceivers. A minimal
PCI interface requires either a silicon foundry or a PCI-qualified FPGA.
