OK I'm still having a few problems getting kermit onto my microVMS
system. For completeness this is a MicroVAX II running MicroVMS 4.1M
(I know it's ancient but there we are ;)

I got hold of VMSMIT.HEX and the hexify/dehexify programs from
micros.hensa.ac.uk which mirrors the kermit site in the US. ftp to the
US is way to slow to use at present BTW (keeps timing out), so I'm
relying on UK mirrors which seem to lack the /kermit/old directory
...

The READ.ME says:

 VMSMIT.HEX        Kermit-32 3.3.128 (runs on VAX/VMS 4.x)

So I figured this should be fine to get going with.

I got hexify/dehexify to work OK and then set to the task of
transferring VMSMIT.HEX to the system. In the absence of KERMIT I'm
simply using:

	COPY OPA0: VMSMIT.HEX

and then doing a "text upload" from my terminal emulator on the
PC. Unfortunately, the thing seems to die with an "overrun error" if I
try to send too much in one go! I played with flow control (Xon/Xoff,
RTS/CTS, both, and neither!) and couldn't get round the problem. The
serial port on my DZQ11 (TTA0:) behaves the same way, so it doesn't
seem to be a wierdness in the console port.

Eventually I settled for splitting the file into 25 line chuncks and
transferring each at a mere 1200 baud. I recombined them using COPY
under VMS, and successfully dehexified.

The resulting VMSMIT.EXE will not run, however:

	Image file LIBRTL.EXE
	Ident mismatch with shareable image

So I guess I have a too old version of VMS leading to shared library
problems.

In conclusion: 

1) I need to source to KERMIT32 in Macro32 form, so I can build my own
(working) version!

2) My flow-control/handshaking on the serial lines seems not to be
quite right. The cabling is as follows:

For console port:

uVAX Console --- 9 to 25 way adaptor ---- "Modem" --- Gender  --- IBM PC
Port (9 pin)                               cable      changer     DB25

For DZQ11 port:

DZQ11 port --- "Modem" --- "Null modem" --- IBM PC
(25 pin)        cable       cable           DB25

The "input overrun" seems to occur on both lines ... what gives?

Cheers,

Bob.

