noel@npt.nuwc.navy.mil (Thomas W. Noel, Jr. AMA) wrote:

>Second Star Coupler

>We are in the process of bringing a second Star Coupler into our VAXCluster
>The first Star Coupler, as well as the second, are configured for 16 nodes.  
>Our current VAXCluster consists of six nodes (3 7620's, 2 8820's, 1 8600's), 
>6 HSC 95's, and 4 CMD Hawk SCSI controllers.  The CI node numbers will be 
>identical on both Star Couplers, the HSC's and Hawk's will appear only on one 
>Star Coupler. We will bringing 4 HSJ40's into the CI on the second Star Coupler.
>Is anyone in this configuration or tried to go to this configuration?  Should 
>this be done while the cluster is shut down?  Can it be done "on the fly?"  Are
>there any unique circumstances or problems we should be aware of?

>Thanks for your help...

>Tom (NOEL@NPT.NUWC.NAVY.MIL)



As far as I am aware, the Star Coupler is merely a junction box, and
has abosolutely NO effect on the working of the cluster.  You are
still limited by the number of direct CI nodes (16 if my memory serves
me correctly) however, and this must be adhered to.  In terms of
adding in another Star Coupler "on-the-fly", DON'T EVEN THINK ABOUT
IT.  This will corrupt all your data and may even damage some of the
CI devices.  This must be done when all nodes are stut down and
POWERED OFF.

The process of adding axtra Start Couplers is very easy and should not
cause you any real problems, as long as everything is cleanly shutdown
and powered off.  I have done similar tasks to this on several
occasions without any real problem,

Hope this helps

Ashley




