In article <4ffgh0$o78@paperboy.ids.net>, cormack_pl@ids.net (Michael Umbricht) writes:
>I am trying to get an LN03A printer going.  When I print a test page the left
>half of the page looks fine but the right half is completely black.  The test
>page shows "Job Status: No Errors" and the output matches the sample in the
>manual.
>
>Before I buy a maintenance kit - is this problem something that will go away
>with a new developer or is there something broken?  Any way I can test it?

Open the left side door on the printer, and remove the toner collection bottle. 
Behind the bottle is a green tab connected to one of the chargers.  Pull the
charger out.  There is another charger accessible from the left side door,
which is upside-down relative to the one you just pulled out.  The two chargers
are identical.  Try swapping them.

Next to the upper charger is a shield glass with a green tab.  Pull it out, and
make sure it's clean.  This glass is there primarily to keep the toner out of
the optics, and if it gets dirty, can cause print quality problems.

If this causes a change in your printout, then get the maintenance kit.  The
kit comes with a new OPC belt, 2 chargers and a new lens/shield glass.

>I also noticed that normal laser printer paper jams when it is being fed.  The
>manual recommends DEC paper.  What is different about this paper?  Any readily
>available substitutes?

It's kinda like the manual that came with my car.  They say that they recommend
Motorcraft supplies, but most any good quality supplies should do fine.

When the paper jams, where does it jam?  Is there an 'F' indicated (meaning a
feed jam), or is there an 'E' indicated (meaning an exit jam)?

