In article <4fe8pa$p8d@news-e2a.gnn.com>, JGroleau@gnn.com (Jim Groleau)
writes:

:We are using a communication board at work that is supposed to be Q-bus 
:compatible.  The board manufacturer used a pin on the C connector for the 12 
:volt supply.  This works fine on a microvax 3500 which has the C and D 
:connector wired on the backplane but is incompatible with a VAX 4500 which 
:does not have the C and D connectors wired.  at some point DEC decided to 
:save money and no longer wire the C and D connector.  The question is did the 
:Q-bus specification ever include the C and D connector or just the A and B 
:connector?  Did the Q-bus spec change to eliminate the C and D connectors?
:Specific DEC references would be helpful. Thanks in advance.

  The Q22 specification has included `Q22' and `QCD' (I'm here using `QCD'
  as the name of the interconnect bus located in the `CD' pins as a means
  to clearly differentiate the interconnect from the `CD' pins themselves)
  slots for many years, and these slots have existed in enclosures such as
  the BA23, dating back as far as the PDP-11/23+ (if not before).

  There are several Q-bus enclosures available:

	BA23:  Q22/QCD in AB/CD through slot 3,
		with serpentine Q22/Q22 in AB/CD starting at slot 4.
	BA123: Q22/QCD in AB/CD through slot 4,
		with serpentine Q22/Q22 at slot 5
	BA2xx/BA4xx: Q22/QCD in AB/CD, full width.

  If a Q22 quad board is doing much of anything other than providing a DMA
  grant jumper in the CD pins (etch from AR2 to AS2; this etch is necessary
  for quad-wide modules installed in Q22/Q22 slots), then the module does
  not appear to be designed for use in a Q22/QCD slot.  This grant isn't
  strictly needed in Q22/QCD slots.

  The QCD bus (CD bus) uses direct-wiring of the pins from one CD slot to
  the next...  Typically only the processor and memory make use the QCD bus,
  but that's not a requirement.  (I highly prefer to avoid having Q22 DMA
  grants in the CD pins, but that is arguably not strictly necessary in
  most cases.  But it's that `odd case' that'll `nail' you... :-)

  Based on your comments, it would appear the board was [mis]designed
  with the assumption that AB2 (-12V) or AD2 (+12V) was also available
  as CB2/CD2.  This holds true while the board is installed in a Q22/Q22
  slot -- it doesn't hold with a Q22/QCD slot.

  Power and ground (and battery backup) pins also exist in the B fingers.

  Documentation: EK-BA200-DG (BA200 module designer's guide), EK-KA630-UG
  (KA630 CPU user's guide), EK-KA680-TM KA680 CPU technical manual).

