In article <4fklo7$kd5@tst.hk.super.net>, jackyh@news.hk.super.net (Mr Jacky Ho) writes:
> Can anyone enlighten me by telling the approx figures of  VAX and ALPHA 
> boxes resp ?   Local DEC support urges us to go ALPHA because it is their 
> corporate trend to drop VAX one day.  We are asking for migration path, a 
> smooth and realistic one.  Who cares what is their corporate decision if 
> my corporation falls down because of VAX ==> Alpha.
> 
> Anyone having similar experience ?  Is the conversion easy ?  Anyone 
> still wants to play around with VAX and have no plans to move to Alpha.  
> In fact, I doubt if VAX will fade away if DEC is producing new boxes 
> likes 78xx series.  Any advice out there ?

The ease of conversion depends on your applications.  The *cost* of conversion
depends on your mix of layered products.  For many applications, in particular
transaction type processing, we've found that the Alpha architecture simply
doesn't the cost of conversion.  $/VUP, the Vax wins.

If you start over from scratch, and neglect the fact that you've already
invested in Vax software licences, the Vax may lose to Alpha.  Given that
Digital won't charge to upgrade layered products from one Vax to a larger one
(like the VAX 7000-800 series, where in fact the layered product unit
requirements *decrease* from 66xx or 76xx Vaxes), but charges a hefty fee to
convert to Alphas, many customers, us included, will stay with the Vax
architecture for a while yet.

Digital obviously realizes that Alphas are not the answer for all customers, or
there would be no need to continue producing Vaxes.

We've documented several cases where a Vax 76xx uses less CPU to do the same
job as an Alpha 2100 @ 190Mhz.  Our 77xx kick butt, and we don't even have a
78xx yet.  For application compiles, we've found that the 2100 can use 4-5
times as much CPU as the 76xx.  The 76xx is not 4x the price of the 2100.  In
number-crunching applications, I believe the Alpha architecture may be cost
justified.  In commercial applications, the Vax is the way to go for now.

This reminds me of the VAX 94xx fiasco - it sure looked good on paper, but it
sure stunk for our applications.  A VUP definitely isn't a VUP when it comes to
those lemons.

As long as customers demand Vax cycles, Digital will continue to build and sell
Vaxes.  After all, if they make money, why stop?  Digital's only hope in
phasing out Vaxes would be to offer the Vax -> Alpha licenses at reasonable
cost.  I personally don't feel 50% of the list price is reasonable.  As long as
the product is on maintenance there should be no upgrade cost, just as there
isn't an upgrade cost to move to larger Vaxes.

