        Well, not for EDT.  Er, um, EDT isn't supported anymore, is it?

        If you would be willing to  (plug  your ears now, folks) move to
    EVE (aka TPU), and use the EDT keypad, then the answer would be yes.

        Digital sells a layered  product  that includes a spell checker,
    but  since I've never used it, I can't tell you the product name.  A
    digit ought to be able to do that for you.  However,  you  can  also
    get  the  DECUS/Vassar Spell program, which is freely available from
    most good VMS ftp sites, e.g., ftp.wku.edu.  Spell works on both VAX
    and Alpha hardware, and  it  works  with  EVE's  SPELL command (once
    you've  placed  a  dummy LNGSPLCOR.EXE in  SYS$SYSTEM  or  otherwise
    appropriately defined LNGSPLCOR as a logical  name  pointing  to  an
    executable, the Spell documentation tells how to do this).

        Both the Digital product and  the  DECUS Spell program work with
    EVE.   You should have your users who want the feature to add a line
    to their LOGIN.COM, $ DEFINE EVE$KEYPAD EDT, so  they  get  the  EDT
    keypad  mapping  automatically  when  EVE starts up.  Also have them
    read the help topics (while  within  EVE)  "EDT conversion" and "EDT
    differences".

