   Well, I've been trying to get my MicroVAX II up and running... here's a
   recap...

Just got my MicroVAX II (2 of 'em) going in the last week or so, so
we're in the same boat here. I'm new to VAX/VMS, and have needed a lot
of net help to get the beast off the ground, so to speak ;)

   Neither of the hard drives seemed to have a bootable OS, hanging
   forever at startup test #2 (the boot OS one). My TK-50 drive isn't
   talking to the system--I am trying to boot VMS from said drive.

Check the controller cabling carefully. Are these internal or external
drives? Are you using the RXRQ (or whatever it's called) controller -
a full width DEC affair, or a 3rd party controller. My systems use
Emulex q32 (I think) MSCP controllers and have external drives. I know
about MSCP (a little) having got that going ... let me know what you
have. TK50s are very fussy devices. Is a fault indication being shown
(rapidly flashing red light)? What are the lights on the TK50 showing?

   Now, after swapping some boards around, I get the following error when
   trying to access any device: ?4C DEVINACT. This, apparently, means that
   none of my devices are online. At least now I'm getting an error, but
   my questions are:

?DEVINACT and other messages seem to be issued from the MicroVAX II
with no discernable pattern when things are wrong. My advice would be
to remove all but the essential boards and build up from a base that
is known to be working. Start with:

Slot 1: CPU (KA630 v1.3 ??)
Slot 2: Memory -- don't forget the PMI ribbon cable
Slot 3: M9047 (?) grant cty cards
Slot 4: Disk controller

See how that fares. If that doesn't work, try the TK50 instead of the
disks ... you may need to check CSRs and vectors for the boards. I
have _some_ but not all, of the docs at home...

   1) Is there a console command that can tell me what the CPU cam see,
   device-wise?

No, AFAIK.

   2) What might cause the devices to all be invisible to the CPU?

Bad CSRs, bad vectors, 'gaps' in the use of slots, missing grant cty
cards. SOmething misconfigured ... check everything methodically.

   3) This is sure to be a let-down, but how fast is a MicroVAX II anyway?
   Speed in Mhz doesn't seem too useful here... can someone compare it to
   another, more familiar system? I mean, is my ancient Apple //gs gonna
   blow past it? (Hey, I love the old hardware...)

Not very fast, but apparently faster CPUs may be fitted
easily. Officially the MicroVAX II is 0.9 of a VAX11/780 MIP - that is
a regualr MIP to you or I. The Apple //gs is gonna be a bit slower
than that I'd have though. I think we're talking IBM at territory,
unless someone wants to correct me ...

Good luck,

Bob.
