organization: Moene Computational Physics, Maartensdijk, The Netherlands
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

In article <1057@moene.indiv.nluug.nl> I wrote:

[ ... Segmentation fault on a program using 16 Mb of stack
      when 40 Mb is allowed - under Digital Unix 3.2C ... ]
> 
> [ Local DEC support couldn't come up with an answer too, yet ]
>

Well, today they did.  The answer is that Digital Unix 3.2C as shipped has  
the setting of the kernel variable 'stackinc' wrong.  It is shipped as 4  
pages (32 Kb) and should be 16 pages (128 Kb).  How exactly this leads to  
the problem described I don't know; nor do I know if in cases of even  
larger stack usage the quoted number of 16 pages is adequate.

As root, perform the following:

# kdbx -k /vmunix /dev/mem
p stackinc
32767
a stackinc=0x20000
131072
quit

Normally, the automatic growth of the stack is made possible by bounding  
it with 'guard' pages (in fact, page table entries that will lead to  
access violations giving the OS the chance to expand the stack area).   
Apparently, there's a mismatch between the amount of expansion the OS  
thinks it has performed and the real value.  This, however, is a guess - I  
haven't seen any kernel code of Digital Unix; perhaps someone from Digital  
in the know could clarify.

--
