In article <00001be4+0000157f@msn.com>, wtutay@msn.com (william tutay) writes:
|> I have a PDP11/53 running RSX11 and an ALPHA running OpenVms on a 
|> DECNET network -- sorry for not being more specific, I'm new to VMS 
|> and DECNET.
|> thanks in advance for any hekp anyone can offer.

Odds are that the preferred method for your specific situation would
be to use DECnet.

Easiest would be to type the command
COPY node"user passwd"::srcfile.nam destfile.nam
on your VMS system.  Things could be a tad more complicated if you
need to push from the RSX side, the native DECnet command would be
NFT node/user/pass::destfile.nam=srcfile.nam
which as shown uses a slightly different syntax.

The only major gotchas might be buffering size matching and perhaps
record typing but I believe they are both more likely to be problems
when the RSX node is the target.  Copying files over the net to the
RSX system can be a bit touchy, PDPs are a little older and not quite
as refined (put less kindly, they're a few bits shy of a quad-word :-)
so the DECnet implementation isn't always as transparent as it might
be.  Hopefully you won't run into such problems, if you do the best
advice is to play around with different combinations of push and pull,
switches, etc.  Worst I've encountered recently was a case that ended
up using RMSCNV to fix up a record type on the VMS side that the RSX
side couldn't digest.  Use the net, but rely on your ingenuity (and
pay homage to invention's mother! :-).

--bruce mcculley
  long time RSX developer, still sometimes hacking 16 bit systems



