In article <823377715.23012@howie.demon.co.uk>, howie@howie.demon.co.uk says...
>
>I'm trying to connect a Digital Workstation monitor to a PC.
>It is a VRT19-HA
>I understand it is a fixed Frequency monitor and can handle
>resolutions of 1280x1024 and 1024x768.
>Horizontal scan rate 54khz
>Vertical refresh rate 60 Hz
>
>Connection is via 3 (RGB) BNC's
>
>-----------------------------------------------------------------
>
>I'm driving it with a Diamond Stealth 64 Vram 15 pin D connector.
>
>The Stealth can drive at both 1024x768 60Hz and 1280 1024 60Hz.
>
>-----------------------------------------------------------------
>
>I've tried woth a 15pin to (RGB) BNC without too much success so
>far, I'm hoping it's just the lead and therefor need to know
>the connections I need within it.
>
>Therefore is there anyone that can help me with this, either that
>has done it before or that can let me know the correct connections ?
>
>

Well, you need to correct a couple things:

The frequency information for the DEC VRT19-HA monitor is as follows:

Horizontal Frequency:		70 or 77 Khz
Vertical Frequency:		60-72 Hz
Video Signal Input:		RGB with Sync-on-Green

Obviously the problem is your video card: It is not capable
of supporting the 1280x1024@70 Khz+ and can not "generate" 
sync-on-green.

What you need is a PC video card that will provide what your 
monitor needs to become a multi-resolution, PC compatible
unit. 

Check the MIRAGE home page at:

http:/www.lainet.com/mirage/

or for the DEC monitors check the:

http:/www.lainet.com/mirage/dec.htm

for a video card that has been designed to perform such a task.





