In Article <jdesbien-2708561955540001@tty39.lino.com>
jdesbien@lino.com (Jean Desbiens) writes:
>Hi all,
>
>I just got my new AlphaStation 400 running under DEC Unix (OSF-1) and I
>keep on typing the same command over and over. Is there a utility that
>provides an history of recent command that can invoke them by hitting the
>UP arrow on the keyboard (as in OpenVMS)?

This depends on the shell that you are using.  UNIX supports several
shells (i.e. the functional equivalent of DCL).  You can usually tell
what shell you are using by doing

echo $SHELL
-or-
echo $shell

If you are using /bin/csh, then you can enable command line editing by
doing the following:

set editmode=emacs
-or-
set editmode=vi

(You'd probably want to use the emacs form since that enables arrow keys.)

If you are using /bin/ksh the equivalent command would be:

set -o emacs
-or-
set -o vi

With the additional wrinkle that if you set the variable EDITOR to include
the string emacs, gmacs or vi, then that editing style will be used instead.
As above, the "emacs" version will get you use of your arrow keys.

