John w Pruitt (pruittj@world.std.com) wrote:


: Hello,
: Last night, I went to my project management class
: I was told by a computer security expert who works
: for the Army that unix computers can not be attacked by 
: viruses.

He's misinformed.  UNIX is not immune to viruses.  Oddly enough, the
guys at Bell Labs who wrote UNIX were mischevous folks and three
papers about viruses unleased by them inside Bell Labs have been
published in the Scientific Literature:

Ken Thompson, "Reflections on Trusting Trust." Communications of the
ACM.  Volume 27, Number 8. (August 1984).  This is an reprint of
Thompson's 1983 Turing Award Lecture.  It discusses a virus inserted
in the C compiler and system login programs on UNIX.  This got around
inside Bell Labs for Ken claims was not delivered to NSA which got the
first US Government UNIX distribution.

Tom Duff, "Experience with Viruses on UNIX Computing Systems."
Computing Systems.  Volume 2 Number 2 (Spring 89).  This is a
more detailed description of his Winter USENIX 89 paper on a
virus he was playing around with inside the lab that nearly got
loose into a UNIX distribution.

M. Douglas McIllroy, "Virology 101" (same issue of Computing Systems
as Duff's paper).  Just when you thought that you couldn't intruduce
a virus in a shell script...

---

I used to be on the systems staff of a UNIX computing facility at
a University in the early days of UNIX.  I can remember one security
attack that can properly be termed a virus.

: So I mentioned, what about the worm
: That attacked the internet.  He said that Virus
: is a Virus and Worm is a Worm.  There like 
: Apples and Oranges he said.

It is, but actually the government characturized the Morris Worm
as virus (which I disagree with).

: And what computer Language is unix written in.  I
: have always assumed it was written in C. 

Mostly C, not that it makes any difference.

The reason viruses aren't very prevelent in UNIX probably is
because of the diverse nature of UNIX platforms and non-commercial
software is usually distributed in source code form.  PC's are
plentiful and largly homogeneous making them much more attractive
targets for viruses.

If you are solely interested in creating havoc on UNIX machines, viruses
have never been the easy vector to infect things.  Most UNIX security
is leaky as a sieve.  It's been an odd system that I couldn't get
root in an hour or so of concentrated hacking.



