noel@npt.nuwc.navy.mil (Thomas W. Noel, Jr. AMA) wrote:
>Second Star Coupler
>
>We are in the process of bringing a second Star Coupler into our VAXCluster
>The first Star Coupler, as well as the second, are configured for 16 nodes.  
>Our current VAXCluster consists of six nodes (3 7620's, 2 8820's, 1 8600's), 
>6 HSC 95's, and 4 CMD Hawk SCSI controllers.  The CI node numbers will be 
>identical on both Star Couplers, the HSC's and Hawk's will appear only on one 
>Star Coupler. We will bringing 4 HSJ40's into the CI on the second Star Coupler.
>Is anyone in this configuration or tried to go to this configuration?  Should 
>this be done while the cluster is shut down?  Can it be done "on the fly?"  Are
>there any unique circumstances or problems we should be aware of?
>
>Thanks for your help...
>
>Tom (NOEL@NPT.NUWC.NAVY.MIL)
>
>
>

There should be no problems doing what you describe.  The hardware rules 
are the same, the node numbers have to be unique within the star and the 
tick count and cluster size settings have to match on all the nodes 
connected to the star.

There should be no problem with doing a rolling install on the second CI 
adapter.  You will of course have to shut each system down to install the 
second CI adapter, but a complete cluster shutdown and reboot shouldn't 
be needed unless you are doing things like changing allocation classes. 

The controller names have to be unique within the cluster.  You can't 
call an HSC95 "RED" on one star and an HSJ40 "RED" on the other.

The disk unit numbers have to be unique within an allocation class.  You 
can't have $1$dua0 on both the HSC95 and the HSJ40 even though they are 
on different star couplers.  If you do, VMS will get confused and think 
that they are the same disk.  Having a $1$dua0 and a $2$dua0 is OK.

You MAY need to increase SCSCONNCNT (but probably not given the size of 
cluster you listed as VMS adds 200 to whatever your sysgen value is).


