I discovered ANALYZE/DISK yesterday. I had previously looked in the
MicroVMS manuals (no mention of ANALYZE) and the help pages (no
mention of ANALYZE/DISK, only a couple of other switches). Leaving
switches off the help pages is a dirty trick!

Anyhow ran ANALYZE/DISK/REPAIR. It found a few things, including some
INCORRECT BACKLINKS which it couldn't correct. I eventually looked
into these and found that one file was referenced twice
(DUA0:[000000.SYSEXE]SYSBOOT.EXE & DUA0:[000000.SYS0.SYSEXE]SYSBOOT.EXE) 
I fixed this by hand by removing the file, fixing the inconsistency with 
ANALYZE/DISK and reinstating the file in both places. There was also
a directory or two with INCORRECT BACKLINKS - I fixed them too.

I now discover (this morning) that I should have ignored those error
messages since they were due to `aliases' on the disk. Seems like UNIX
links but implemented rather hackishly. 

****
Question is, is this important, and, if so, how do I reinstate said
aliases? Also can anyone tell me what _should_ be aliased to
what? 
****

Providing a disk structure that the integrity checking utility thinks
is illegal is rather nasty IMHO.

Cheers,

Bob.
