Greetings, gentlebeings.

  First, forgive me for crossposting this so wildly; however, this is kind of
an "interdisciplinary" hardware/software/networking problem and I don't know
exactly WHERE the network gurus hang out!  (If I should have posted this
somewhere OTHER than comp.os.vms, comp.sys.dec, and comp.windows.nt.*, PLEASE
let me know so I can put it where it's more appropriate.)

  Second, I am a regular reader ONLY of comp.os.vms, of all the groups I've
cross-posted this to; I'll TRY to keep up with responses for whatever time
period seems to apply, but Portal doesn't retain articles long and I may well
miss some of your responses.  Therefore, could I please ask you to "e-mail me 
too, even if you ALSO POST a response in the newsgroup(s)?"  I'll happily do 
the same for you if YOU make such a request in comp.os.vms or any of the other
groups where I AM a regular reader.  Thanks in advance.

   Having said all of that, here's the situation.

   For the past eight years my company has made a realtime device which, in
previous incarnations, has operated in tandem with a VMS host computer.  These
previous incarnations contained an embedded 68000-based single-board computer,
and communicated with the VMS host via a DRV-11 link, using a proprietary
protocol implemented in a custom device driver of our own making.  The "next
generation" product is almost completely different: it contains an embedded
Alpha-based host single-board computer (the Alpha follow-on to the "KAV30"
VAX-based single-board computer; I can't remember the $%#$&^^ Alpha-based
product's MODEL number!!!), and communicates using a variant of the same
proprietary protocol, layered atop TCP/IP and Ethernet transport and physical
"link" layers.  The host computer is now an AlphaStation 4/233 running Windows
NT 3.5.1.  It works fine, but we're having a "political" problem which we
have to solve through "technical" means.

   To wit, we're trying to go through "CE" ("Community Europe," equivalent
to the FCC in the U.S., but "more strict," I'm told).  I'm told that we're
failing some kind of RF leakage test -- I'm not sure of the specifics as I'm 
summarizing and posting this on behalf of someone else who's shepherding the
actual testing -- at 144 MHz.  I'm told that "the limit is 30 dB, but our
Ethernet/TCP/IP link [is producing] 38 dB."  The AlphaStation, itself, is
CE-approved -- i.e., if it were just sitting there by itself, NOT communicating
with our device, it would pass the RF-leakage etc. test -- so it's something
specifically related to either the Ethernet link, or the single-board embedded
system in our device, or both.

   We'd presume that Digital, itself, has already long ago had to deal with
this "CE standards testing," and we're hoping they had this problem, or one
like it, and were able to solve it, and we'd like to know HOW they solved it. 
Is there any Digital product using TCP/IP for communication, that passes the 
test we're failing?

   We're kind of "grasping at straws" at this point, so ANY tidbit of wisdom
you can dispense would be much appreciated.  Thanks in advance!

   Chris Chiesa
     Chris_F_Chiesa@cup.portal.com / xetwnk@shell.portal.com



