Hi All,

I hope that someone recognises the symptoms I'll describe in a moment and  
is able to point me to a solution.

I _was_ the happy user of an Alphaserver (don't know the exact model  
number - it has 2 190 Mhz Alpha 21064's, 192 Mb of memory and several  
gig's of disk space) and was running Digital Unix 3.2.

Then, on a black Tuesday, system management decided to upgrade to 3.2C and  
- lo and behold - several of my program binaries, which had been running  
unattented for weeks (one to three hours at a stretch), stopped working  
with a Segmentation fault.

The problem was easily reproduced by the following simple program:

      program bug
      real a(4000000)
      do i = 1, 4000000
         a(i) = 0.0
      enddo
      print*,(a(i), i=1,4000000,100000)
      end

when compiled with f77 -automatic, or the equivalent C program:

main()
{
	int i;
	float a[4000000];
	for (i=0; i<4000000; i++)
		a[i] = 0.0;
	printf("%f\n", a[1000000]);
}

(The print statements are only there to prevent all code to be optimised  
away - they're never reached).

The Fortran program can be made running by omitting the '-automatic'  
switch, the C program by adding 'static' to the declaration of the array.

And yes, the 'limit' command shows I am allowed to use up to 40 Mb of  
stack space.

Any takers ?

TIA.

[ Local DEC support couldn't come up with an answer too, yet ]

