What type of Ethernet/TCP-IP link are you using?  My understanding is that
conventional unshielded twisted pair does NOT meet the new CE specs.
Your best bet is to use thin Ethernet (make sure that the cards have the
BNC connectors as a short twisted pair stub to a converter will radiate).

If you are already using thin Ethernet, you may have to replace the
isolated BNC connector with a special BNC connector that has bypass
capacitors between the shield and the frame.

If you are using 100 mbits/sec Ethernet, you may have to use fiber optics
as I am not aware of any shielded 100 mbits/sec copper Ethernet media.

In general, make sure that all your cables are fully shielded and that the
shield is connected to the frames at both end (directly or using special
connectors with bypass capactitors, conventional capacitors have too much
inductance).

A band-aid solution that may help is to place a ferrite core at each end
of the cable near the connectors (you can get clamp on ferrite cores that
can be added to an existing cable).   These are frequently used on VGA
cables.

What sort of enclosure are you using for your single-board embedded CPU?
Make sure it is electrically conductive and no slots or holes longer than
2 or 3 in.  Use EMI gaskets if necessary.  Make sure the power connector
is filtered (Corcom or equivalent).

It may take some trial and error to obtain a satisfactory solution.
A good book is: "Noise Reduction Techniques in Electronic Systems", 2nd ed,
by Henry W. Ott (John Wiley & Sons).

